The skills shortage in STEM fields is a binding constraint on innovation and growth in most developed economies — free education increases STEM graduate supply by removing the risk of debt for potentially lower-paying academic careers.
High student debt reduces consumer spending and entrepreneurship among graduates — two of the key drivers of innovation-led growth.
Research universities are economic anchors: the Boston metro area, Silicon Valley, and Cambridge UK each built $1 trillion+ economies largely around the gravitational pull of their universities.
Brain drain — educated graduates emigrating to countries where they can earn debt-free returns on their education — is a measurable cost to countries with high tuition; free education reduces this emigration incentive.
Public investment in education has a higher social return than private investment because the graduate's skills benefit employers, customers, and communities beyond the individual — this positive externality is the textbook case for public subsidy.
